|~ `::qlang`, the topic of the language, and what is declared under it:
   its page, the first screen a session reads, its chapters, and their
   laws.
   The decisions it exists for: [D114], [D185]. ~|

:qlang
  |~~ qlang is a pipeline language over values. A query starts from a
      subject, the input it is given, and passes it through steps: each
      step takes the value the step before answered and answers a new
      one, and the last answer is the answer of the query. Nothing
      changes in place. Everything the language handles is a value with
      a literal that prints and reads back as itself, data, code, prose
      and failures alike, so the language describes itself in its own
      values: every name has a page, the pages grow from ::qlang, and a
      query reads them as it reads any data.

      1. The pipe. `a | b` runs b on the value a answered, its subject:
      `[3 1 2] | sort | first` answers `1`. Each step is a command, a
      word and what it takes, and a value stands where it is the
      subject, at the head or after a declaration, or as what a word
      takes:
      `get /users | filter /active | sort /age | map /name` reads the
      users, keeps the active ones, ranks them by age and answers their
      names. A value written as a later step is refused.

      2. Values. Every value has a kind, a tag that `type` answers:
      `[1 2] | type` answers `::vec`. A value is written as its literal,
      which opens with the sign of its kind and closes with its pair, and
      everything between is literal: a literal reads no name and no
      subject, so it is the value it writes. Numbers, ::number, are
      finite doubles: `42`, `-1.5`. Strings, ::string: `"text"`.
      Keywords, ::keyword, are names that stand for themselves: `:name`.
      Booleans and null: `true`, `false`, `null`. Vectors, ::vec:
      `[1 2 3]`. Maps, ::map, have keyword keys: `{:a 1 :b 2}`. Sets,
      ::set, hold no duplicates and keep one order: `#[3 1 3]` answers
      `#[1 3]`. A tag written before a literal puts the value under it,
      `::Box{:w 2}`, a number, a boolean, null or a path touching it by
      `#`, `::Width#5`. JSON is a literal too and pastes into a query as
      it is, its keys read as keywords: `{"a": [1, 2]}` answers
      `{:a [1 2]}`. A keyword is not its string: `:a | eq "a"` answers
      `false`, and `toString` and `toKeyword` turn one into the other,
      `:a | toString` answering `"a"`.

      3. Reading parts. A path, ::path, names a part of a value: `/k` a
      key, `/0` an element, `/-1` the last, `/a/b/0` a part at depth. A
      path is a value and does nothing by itself; the word that takes it
      reads it. `get` reads it and refuses a part that is not there:
      `{:a {:b [10 20]}} | get /a/b/-1` answers `20`. `query` reads it
      softly and answers null where the part is missing, or the default
      it is given: `{:a 1} | query /b 0` answers `0`. `has /b` asks
      whether the part is there, and `it` is the value itself.

      4. Commands. A command is a word and what it takes, separated by
      spaces: `add 1`, `split ","`, `take 2`, `get /price`. The word is
      found through the kind of the subject: `count` counts the elements
      of a vector and the characters of a string. Every modifier is
      computed once, at the call, against the subject of the step, and
      the word receives the values: `10 | add 3` answers `13`. The
      subject of a step is what the step before it answered, so in
      `{:price 4 :qty 3} | get /price | mul (get /qty)` the `/qty` is
      read from the price, a number with no parts, and the call is
      refused, `::path/SubjectNotProjectableError`: two values of one
      record are read in one step, `get [/price /qty]`, or combined by a
      word that reads them, `{:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty`
      answering `12`. A modifier of more than one word stands in
      parentheses: `[1 2 3] | take (count | sub 1)` answers `[1 2]`.

      5. Code. A quote, `~(…)`, is code, and a quote computes to itself,
      so the word receives it unrun and runs it where it needs. `map`
      runs its code on each element, that element its subject:
      `[1 2 3] | map ~(mul 2)` answers `[2 4 6]`. A path is code too, the
      code that reads it from each element: `[{:n 2} {:n 1}] | sort /n`
      answers `[{:n 1} {:n 2}]`. `filter` keeps the elements its code
      holds of, `[1 2 3] | filter ~(gt 1)` answering `[2 3]`; `groupBy`
      files the elements under what its code answers, or under
      themselves, `["b" "a" "b"] | groupBy | map ~(count)` answering
      `{:b 2 :a 1}`; `reduce` folds the elements into a seed, calling
      its word with the value so far as the subject and the element as
      the modifier, `[1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(add)` answering `6`, and
      running code of several steps on the pair of the two,
      `[{:n 1} {:n 2}] | reduce 0 ~(sumOf /0 /1/n)` answering `3`.
      Several steps stand in one quote, `map ~(get /price | mul 2)`, and
      `apply` runs its code on the subject itself,
      `5 | apply ~(add 1 | mul 2)` answering `12`. Without the quote, a
      group is a modifier computed at the call: in
      `[{:n 1}] | map (get /n)` the `/n` is read from the vector itself,
      not from each element, and `map` refuses what it is handed,
      `::vec/map/code/NotPathOrQuoteError`.

      6. Building answers. A literal computes nothing, so an answer of a
      new shape is built by `get` over a template, a literal of paths and
      quotes, each path read and each quote run against the subject:
      `{:first "Ann" :age 30} | get {:who /first :next ~(get /age | add 1)}`
      answers `{:who "Ann" :next 31}`, and `{:a 1 :b 2} | get [/b /a]`
      answers `[2 1]`; `query` reads the paths of a template softly. A
      group or a name written inside a literal is refused, and a value
      computed goes under a tag by `tag`, `1 | add 2 | tag ::Box`.

      7. Names. A step sees only the value handed to it: after
      `get /qty |` the subject is a number, and the record it came from
      is gone unless a name keeps it. `:r it` names the value in the pipe
      `r`, and `:n (…)` names what its group computes; the value in the
      pipe goes on unchanged, and the bare word reads the name back, `:r`
      staying a keyword:
      `{:qty 2 :price 3} | :r it | get /qty | mul (r | get /price)`
      answers `6`. A name holds code as it holds any value, and a word
      that takes code takes the name bare:
      `:double ~(mul 2) | 5 | apply double` answers `10`, and
      `:double ~(mul 2) | [1 2 3] | map double` answers `[2 4 6]`.
      Declared under the label of the values it serves, the code is a
      word the name calls, `:number/double ~(mul 2) | 5 | double`
      answering `10`, which in the reader's own queries shadows a word of
      that name on a broader label, `::any` among them, and which no word
      of the root sees. A name is declared once in a scope, and the names
      a group or a quote declares stay in it.

      8. Elements and order. `count`, `sum`, `avg`, `min`, `max`,
      `first` and `last` read the elements of a vector, a set or a map.
      A map's elements are its values, the keys travelling with them:
      `{:a 1 :b 5} | filter ~(gt 2)` answers `{:b 5}`, and
      `{:a 1 :b 5} | map ~(add 1)` answers `{:a 2 :b 6}`. `keys` answers
      its keys, and `entries` the pairs `[key value]`, which `toMap`
      builds back into a map. One order ranks every value, so anything
      sorts: null, then booleans, numbers, strings, keywords, vectors,
      sets, maps. `desc` turns a key to descend:
      `sort ~(get [~(get /n | desc) /name])` ranks by n descending, then
      by name.

      9. Conditions. A condition is a boolean, and nothing else is
      truthy: `filter` and `if` refuse any other value. `if` takes the
      condition as a modifier and its two branches as code:
      `5 | if (gt 0) ~("pos") ~("neg")` answers `"pos"`. Conditions of
      one value hold together by `allOf` and `anyOf`, whose modifiers
      are computed at the call, every one of them, so a condition that
      may fail where an earlier one does not hold is written as a quote
      that answers it in its turn:
      `filter ~(allOf (get /email | ne null) ~(get /email | endsWith ".org"))`.

      10. Text. A string splits into a vector and joins back,
      `"a,b,c" | split "," | join "-"` answering `"a-b-c"`; `lines`
      breaks text into its lines, and `trim`, `lower`, `upper`,
      `contains`, `startsWith` and `endsWith` read it. A string is never
      read as a number behind the reader's back: `toNumber` reads one,
      `"42" | toNumber | add 1` answering `43`. A pattern, `#"…"`, is
      written as the engine reads it, no backslash doubled, and reads
      text: `"took 12ms" | match #"(\d+)ms" | get /1` answers `"12"`,
      and `capture` answers the groups it names as a record.

      11. Failures. A step that fails answers a failure, a value under
      the tag of what refused: `"x" | add 1 !| type` answers
      `::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError`. `|` passes a failure by, and
      `!|` alone catches it, its step running on the error at rest, a
      value like any other: `"x" | add 1 !| 0` answers `0`. No value
      holds a failure, so a template whose quote fails answers the
      failure, while an error at rest, written `!{:k 1}`, sits anywhere
      and `raise` raises it. A failure carries its facts and its trail, the steps it passed
      with their subjects, and prints as an alert: the subject where it
      arose cut to an excerpt, and each subject it passed an ::elision,
      whose `:read` continues the query to it, `!| get /trail/1/subject`.
      `!| explain` answers the page of what refused above the failure,
      the page saying how to repair the call.

      12. Asking. `::vec | manifest` answers what lies under a kind, its
      words, its refusals and the roles of its chain, and `map` asks
      several kinds at once, each beside its own:
      ::snippet~([::vec ::map ::string ::number ::any] | map ~(get [~(it) ~(manifest)]))
      lists the words most queries use. `::vec/sort | doc` answers the
      page of a word, and `::vec/sort | laws` the laws of a word by
      name, each a page and a query that answers true; `x | type | doc`
      answers the page of the kind of any value. An answer prints whole;
      `x | elide 4000` bounds it to four thousand characters of its
      print, each part left out an ::elision whose `:read` continues the
      query to it.

      13. A task. Over
      `{"users": [{"name": "Ann", "age": 30, "tags": ["a", "b"]}, {"name": "Bo", "age": 15, "tags": []}]}`,
      `get /users | filter ~(get /age | gte 18) | map ~(get {:name /name :n ~(get /tags | count)})`
      answers `[{:name "Ann" :n 2}]`. Look at the input before shaping
      it, `paths` its outline, `keys`, `get /0`, `type`, `count`, and
      build the query a step at a time, running each.

      14. The chapters, in the order they rest on each other:
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc) values, kinds and tags;
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc) the steps of a query;
      ::link~(::qlang/names | doc) names and their records;
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | doc) the words that live on kinds;
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc) the failure as a value;
      ::link~(::qlang/code | doc) code as a value;
      ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | doc) the pages and how to move through
      them;
      ::link~(::qlang/hosts | doc) the tool the language runs in and the
      budget of an answer.
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | proves :runsAGroup
        |~~ A group runs its steps on the value in the pipe. ~~|
        ~(5 | (add 1 | mul 2) | eq 12)
    | proves :buildsAShapeByATemplate
        |~~ A template of `get` builds a shape from the value, its paths
            read and its quotes run on it.
        ~~|
        ~({:first "Ann" :age 30} | get {:who /first :next ~(get /age | add 1)} | eq {:who "Ann" :next 31})
    | proves :buildsTheShapeOfEachElement
        |~~ Under `map` a template builds the shape of each element. ~~|
        ~([{:id 1 :tags [:a :b]}] | map ~(get {:id /id :n ~(get /tags | count)}) | eq [{:id 1 :n 2}])
    | proves :walksAPath
        |~~ A path walks keys and indices, `-1` from the end. ~~|
        ~({:a {:b [10 20]}} | get /a/b/-1 | eq 20)
    | proves :readsAQuotedKey
        |~~ A key with a space is read quoted. ~~|
        ~({"first name": "Ann"} | get /"first name" | eq "Ann")
    | proves :readsATemplateSoftly
        |~~ `query` reads the paths of a template softly. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | query [~(get /a) /b] | eq [1 null])
    | proves :readsSoftly
        |~~ `query` reads a missing key as null. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | query /b | eq null)
    | proves :fallsBack
        |~~ `coalesce` puts a fallback in the place of null. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | query /b | coalesce 0 | eq 0)
    | proves :multipliesTwoFields
        |~~ Two fields of one record multiply by `mulOf`. ~~|
        ~({:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty | eq 12)
    | proves :readsAModifierFromTheSubjectOfItsStep
        |~~ After `get /price |` a modifier is read from the price. ~~|
        ~({:price 4 :qty 3} | get /price | mul (get /qty) !| type | eq ::path/SubjectNotProjectableError)
    | proves :subtractsTwoFields
        |~~ Two fields of one record subtract by `subOf`. ~~|
        ~({:total 10 :paid 3} | subOf /total /paid | eq 7)
    | proves :runsAPipelineInATemplate
        |~~ A pipeline in a template stands in a quote. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | get [~(get /a | add 1) /a] | eq [2 1])
    | proves :losesTheRecordAfterAStep
        |~~ After `get /qty |` a modifier reads the number, not the record. ~~|
        ~({:qty 2 :price 3} | get /qty | mul (get /price) !| type | eq ::path/SubjectNotProjectableError)
    | proves :takesCodeAsAQuote
        |~~ Code a verb runs on each element is a quote. ~~|
        ~([{:age 3} {:age 20}] | filter ~(get /age | gte 18) | count | eq 1)
    | proves :quotesOneWord
        |~~ `~name` quotes one word. ~~|
        ~("[3 1 2] | map ~toString | first" | parse | apply it | eq "3")
    | proves :refusesAValueForCode
        |~~ A value computed at the call is refused where code is wanted.
        ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | filter (gt 1) !| type | eq ::vec/filter/predicate/NotPathOrQuoteError)
    | proves :splitsAndJoins
        |~~ A string splits into a vector and joins back. ~~|
        ~("a,b,c" | split "," | join "-" | eq "a-b-c")
    | proves :cutsAString
        |~~ A string counts, drops and takes by characters. ~~|
        ~("db_host" | get [~(count) ~(drop 3) ~(take 2)] | eq [7 "host" "db"])
    | proves :countsOccurrences
        |~~ `groupBy | map ~count` counts each value. ~~|
        ~(["b" "a" "b"] | groupBy | map ~count | eq {:b 2 :a 1})
    | proves :turnsKeysIntoData
        |~~ Entries turn the keys of a map into data. ~~|
        ~({:auth {:port 3000}} | entries | map ~(unionOf /1 (get {:name ~(get /0 | toString)})) | eq [{:port 3000 :name "auth"}])
    | proves :buildsAMapFromEntries
        |~~ Entries build back into the map. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | entries | toMap | eq {:a 1})
    | proves :folds
        |~~ `reduce` folds the elements from a seed. ~~|
        ~([3 1 2] | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 6)
    | proves :foldsCodeOnThePair
        |~~ Code of several steps folds on the pair of the value so far
            and the element.
        ~~|
        ~([{:n 1} {:n 2}] | reduce 0 ~(sumOf /0 /1/n) | eq 3)
    | proves :ordersASet
        |~~ A set stands in the one order. ~~|
        ~("#[:z :x :y] | first" | parse | apply it | eq :x)
    | proves :answersASetByDistinct
        |~~ `distinct` answers a set. ~~|
        ~([2 3 2 1] | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])
    | proves :answersASetByKeys
        |~~ `keys` answers a set. ~~|
        ~({:y 1 :x 2} | keys | eq #[:x :y])
    | proves :reversesASetIntoAVector
        |~~ `reverse` of a set answers a vector. ~~|
        ~(#[:z :x :y] | reverse | eq [:z :y :x])
    | proves :readsTheValuesOfAMap
        |~~ A map's elements are its values, its keys travelling with
            them.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 5} | filter ~(gt 2) | eq {:b 5})
    | proves :branchesOnABoolean
        |~~ `if` branches on a boolean. ~~|
        ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~("pos") ~("neg") | eq "pos")
    | proves :refusesTruthiness
        |~~ Nothing is truthy: a number is no condition. ~~|
        ~(5 | if 1 ~("yes") ~("no") !| type | eq ::any/if/condition/NotBooleanError)
    | proves :turnsAStringIntoAKeyword
        |~~ `toKeyword` turns a string into a keyword. ~~|
        ~("draft" | toKeyword | eq :draft)
    | proves :turnsAKeywordIntoAString
        |~~ `toString` turns a keyword into its string. ~~|
        ~(:draft | toString | eq "draft")
    | proves :printsText
        |~~ `print` answers the text a value reads back from. ~~|
        ~(7 | print | eq "7")
    | proves :opensAFailureToItsFacts
        |~~ `!|` opens a failure to its facts. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| get /actualType | eq ::string)
    | proves :refusesUnderTheInput
        |~~ An input of none of its kinds is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(1 | add "x" !| type | eq ::number/add/addend/NotNumberError)
    | proves :writesAnError
        |~~ An error is written as a literal, a value whose facts read as
            fields.
        ~~|
        ~(!{:reason :late} | get /reason | eq :late)
    | proves :raisesAMap
        |~~ `raise` builds a failure from a map. ~~|
        ~({:reason :late} | raise !| get [~(type) /reason] | eq [::error :late])
    | proves :writesAnErrorOfItsOwnKind
        |~~ A tag before the bang names the kind of the error. ~~|
        ~(::Late!{:by 3} | get [~(type) /by] | eq [::Late 3])
    | proves :asksWhetherTheStepBeforeStood
        |~~ `ok !| false` asks whether the step before stood. ~~|
        ~(get [~(!{:k 1} | raise | ok !| false) ~(5 | ok !| false)] | eq [false true])
    | proves :holdsTheTrail
        |~~ The trail holds the steps a failure skipped. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| get /trail/-1/skipped | eq ~(mul 2))
    | proves :runsAQuote
        |~~ `apply` runs a quote on a subject. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ~(add 1 | mul 2) | eq 12)
    | proves :parsesText
        |~~ `parse` turns text into a quote. ~~|
        ~("add 1" | parse | eq ~(add 1))
    | proves :declaresAKindOfYourOwn
        |~~ A kind of your own takes a word and an input held to its
            kinds.
        ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A parcel. ~~| | :Box/put (::fn | takes :w |~~ A weight. ~~| /0 ::number | impl ~(within ~(append w))) | ::Box[1] | get [~(put 7) ~(put "x" !| type)] | eq [::Box[1 7] ::Box/put/w/NotNumberError])
    | proves :givesAnInputADefault
        |~~ An input read by `query` has a default. ~~|
        ~(:number/plus (::fn | takes :by |~~ How much, one where none. ~~| ~(query /0 1) | impl ~(add by)) | 5 | get [~(plus) ~(plus 2)] | eq [6 7])
    | proves :runsANamedQuote
        |~~ A quote in a bare name runs by `apply`. ~~|
        ~(:q ~(add 1) | 5 | apply q | eq 6)
    | proves :readsAStepAsACall
        |~~ The step of `add 1` is a `::call` named `add`. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | first | get [~(type) /name] | eq [::call :add])
    | proves :readsADoc
        |~~ A doc splits into its prose and the code it holds. ~~|
        ~(|~~ a ~(eq 1) b ~~| | segments | count | eq 3)
    | proves :looksAtTheInput
        |~~ The keys of the first element show the shape of the input. ~~|
        ~([{:id 1 :tags ["a"]}] | get /0 | keys | eq #[:id :tags])
    | proves :opensTheChapters
        |~~ The links of the first page open its chapters. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | doc | links | map ~open | count | gt 5)
    | tells :values
        |~~ Values. Everything qlang handles is a value, data, code, prose
            and failures alike, so everything the language says about
            itself is a value the pipeline reads. A value never changes,
            and each has a kind, the name of which is a tag: it tells how
            to read the value it stands on, and the tag taken as a value,
            `::Box`, is the kind itself, which a query asks for its page
            and its verbs. Rests on nothing; the pipeline, the verbs and
            the hypertext rest on it.
            ::snippet~(kinds) lists the kinds the scope reads.
            ::link~(::tag | doc)
            ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)

            A literal implies its kind: it opens with the sign of the
            kind and closes with its pair, and everything between is
            literal, so a literal reads neither the scope nor the value in
            the pipe, and a group or a name written inside one is refused.
            A tag written before a literal puts the value under the tag,
            the literal touching it by its own sign, `::Box{:w 2}`,
            `::P[1 2]`, `::T"s"`, `::T:k`, `::T~(add 1)`, and a literal
            whose first character would continue the tag's name, a
            number, `true`, `false`, `null` or a path, by `#`,
            `::Width#5`, `::T#/a`; the constructor of the kind attaches
            the tag to the payload or refuses it, saying why the payload
            does not fit, and builds no other value. `tag` puts the value
            in the pipe under a tag, a value computed among them,
            `1 | add 2 | tag ::Box`; tags stack and read from the outside
            in, `::A::B[1]`, and `payload` takes one off. A step answers a
            new value and the old one stays as it was. Every value prints
            as a literal that reads back as itself. A value is told by how
            its print opens: `"` a string, `:` a keyword, `::` and a name
            alone a tag and with a payload touching it a value under that
            tag, `[` a vector, `#[` a set, `#"` a pattern of text, `{` a
            map, `/` a path, `~(` a quote, `|~~` a doc, `!{` an error at
            rest, a digit or a minus a number, and `true`, `false` and
            `null` themselves; a failure a host prints is its error at
            rest under the line `|~| raised, and no !| caught it`. A kind
            of the core reads a payload of its own kind under its tag as
            the value itself, `::set[2 1]` printing `#[1 2]`.
        ~~|
        #[::tag ::null ::boolean ::number ::string ::regex ::keyword ::path ::vec ::map ::set ::desc ::tagged]
    | proves :values/impliesTheKinds
        |~~ Each literal answers its kind. ~~|
        ~([42 "s" :k [1] {:k 1} #[1] true null] | map ~type | eq [::number ::string ::keyword ::vec ::map ::set ::boolean ::null])
    | proves :values/putsAnyPayloadUnderATag
        |~~ A literal touches its tag by its own sign, and a number, a
            boolean, null or a path by `#`.
        ~~|
        ~([::Box{:a 1} ::Box[1 2] ::Box"s" ::Box:k ::Box#3 ::Box#true ::Box#null ::Box#/a]
          | map ~payload
          | eq [{:a 1} [1 2] "s" :k 3 true null /a])
    | proves :values/attachesTheTagOrRefusesThePayload
        |~~ The constructor of a kind attaches its tag to a payload or
            refuses it.
        ~~|
        ~(:Pos |~~ A number above zero. ~~| (::kind | holds :NotPositiveError |~~ Above zero. ~~| ~(gt 0))
          | get [~(::Pos#3 | payload) ~(::Pos#-3 !| type)]
          | eq [3 ::Pos/NotPositiveError])
    | proves :values/computesNothingInsideALiteral
        |~~ A group or a name inside a literal is refused, and a value
            computed goes under a tag by `tag`.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~("[(1 | add 2)]" | parse !| type) ~(":x 1 | [x]" | parse !| type) ~(1 | add 2 | tag ::Box | payload)]
          | eq [::quote/ParseError ::quote/ParseError 3])
    | proves :values/isToldByHowItsPrintOpens
        |~~ A string, a keyword, a tag, a vector, a set, a pattern, a map,
            a quote and an error at rest each print as their kind opens.
        ~~|
        ~(["a" :a ::A [1] #[1] #"a" {:a 1} ~(a) !{:a 1}] | map ~type | eq [::string ::keyword ::tag ::vec ::set ::regex ::map ::quote ::error])
    | proves :values/readsAKindOfTheCoreUnderItsTag
        |~~ A kind of the core reads its payload under its tag as the
            value itself.
        ~~|
        ~([::set[2 1] ::number#5 ::map{:a 1}] | eq [#[1 2] 5 {:a 1}])
    | proves :values/stacksTags
        |~~ Tags stack and read from the outside in. ~~|
        ~([7 8] | tag ::Inner | tag ::Outer | get [~(type) ~(payload | type) ~(payload | payload)] | eq [::Outer ::Inner [7 8]])
    | proves :values/readsTheOuterTag
        |~~ The type is the outer tag. ~~|
        ~(::Box::Crate{:a 1} | type | eq ::Box)
    | proves :values/changesNothingInPlace
        |~~ Appending leaves the old vector as it was. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | :v it | append 3 | get [~(v) ~(it)] | eq [[1 2] [1 2 3]])
    | proves :values/readsBackItsPrint
        |~~ A value reads back from its print. ~~|
        ~({:a [1 #[2]] :t ::Box[3]} | :v it | print | parse | :q it | apply q | eq v)
    | tells :pipeline
        |~~ The pipeline. A query is a subject and its steps, each a
            command on the value the step before it answered, and the
            answer is the last value; the shape of an answer is written as
            the literal it takes, so a query names what it reads. Rests on
            values; the verbs are its commands, and code holds its steps
            as a value.
            ::link~(::path | doc) ::link~(::group | doc)
            ::link~(::vec/map | doc) ::link~(::qlang/names | doc)

            `|` runs the next step on the answer of the one before. A step
            is a command, and a value stands at the head of a query, of a
            group and of a quote, and after the declarations that open
            one, where it is the subject, or as what a word takes; a value
            written as a later step is refused, and a value goes under a
            tag by `tag`. `map` runs a quote or a path on each element of
            a vector or each value of a map. A pipe written before `!|`
            stands for it. A path, `/k`, `/0`, `/-1`, `/a/b`, is read by
            `get`, and a miss is an error there; `query` reads it softly,
            and `put`, `update` and `delete` write it. A modifier is
            computed against the subject of its own step, the value the
            step before it answered, so two fields of one record are read
            in one step, and a name keeps the record for a later step. A
            name holds the value in the pipe for the steps after it, and
            steps in parentheses run on the value in the pipe and answer
            what their last step answers. A line break is a space, so a
            step on the next line joins by `|`, written at the end of a
            line or at the start of the next, and around a declaration it
            tells where the declaration ends: a keyword that begins a line
            opens a declaration of its own, and the body of a name with a
            path begun on the line after the name runs on through the
            steps joined to it up to the next declaration, so a body of one
            step is written on the line of its name. A comment,
            `|~ … ~|`, nesting, or `|~|` to the end of its line, is
            whitespace too. `|` and `!|` bind alike, from left to right.
            A group is a fork: it starts from the value and the names
            outside it, and only its answer leaves it; a literal computes
            nothing, and the quotes of a template `get` runs are forks of
            their own, so they see nothing of each other.
        ~~|
        #[::path ::group ::bind ::call ::construct]
    | proves :pipeline/runsStepAfterStep
        |~~ Five plus one, times two, is twelve. ~~|
        ~(5 | add 1 | mul 2 | eq 12)
    | proves :pipeline/refusesAValueAsALaterStep
        |~~ A value written as a later step is refused. ~~|
        ~("5 | [1 2]" | parse !| type | eq ::quote/ParseError)
    | proves :pipeline/putsAValueUnderATagByTag
        |~~ A value goes under a tag by `tag`. ~~|
        ~([7 8] | tag ::Box | type | eq ::Box)
    | proves :pipeline/takesAPipeBeforeTheFailTrack
        |~~ A pipe before `!|` stands for it. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | !| 0 | eq 0)
    | proves :pipeline/readsAPath
        |~~ A path reads a nested part. ~~|
        ~({:a {:b 1}} | get /a/b | eq 1)
    | proves :pipeline/failsOnAMiss
        |~~ A miss is an error. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | get /b !| type | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :pipeline/keepsTheRecordByAName
        |~~ A name keeps the record for a later step. ~~|
        ~({:qty 2 :price 3} | :r it | get /qty | mul (r | get /price) | eq 6)
    | proves :pipeline/holdsTheValueByAName
        |~~ A name holds the value in the pipe. ~~|
        ~(5 | :n it | get [~(n) ~(n | add 1)] | eq [5 6])
    | proves :pipeline/joinsLinesByAPipe
        |~~ Steps on several lines join by `|`, a comment between them. ~~|
        ~("[3 1 2]\n|~| the order\n| sort\n| first" | parse | apply it | eq 1)
    | proves :pipeline/joinsNoStepsByALine
        |~~ A line break is a space, and joins no steps. ~~|
        ~("[3 1 2]\nsort" | parse !| type | eq ::quote/ParseError)
    | proves :pipeline/runsADefinitionBegunOnTheNextLineOn
        |~~ The body of a name with a path begun on the line after it runs
            on through the steps joined to it, and the subject passes on.
        ~~|
        ~("[1 2 3] | :vec/f\n  ~(get /1) | count" | parse | apply it | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :pipeline/nestsComments
        |~~ Comments nest and stand where spaces stand. ~~|
        ~("|~ outer |~ inner ~| ~| [1 2] |~ a note ~| | count" | parse | apply it | eq 2)
    | proves :pipeline/seesNothingOfASibling
        |~~ One quote of a template sees nothing of another. ~~|
        ~(get [~(:a 1 | a) ~(a)] !| type | eq ::call/UnknownNameError)
    | tells :names
        |~~ Names. A name holds a value for the steps after it, the one
            way a query remembers: `:n v` declares it in the scope where
            it stands, and a verb and a kind are names alike. A name with
            a path declares a node under the noun its path names before
            its last name, `:Box/area` under `::Box`, a noun declared
            before it, and the tag of a name is its address, `::Box/area`.
            A name is a record, `::binding`, with its page, its source,
            its value and the source it came from, so where a thing was
            declared is read as data. The scope of a step is what the
            steps before it wrote; a step inside `(…)` or a quote a word
            runs writes a scope that closes with it. Under every scope
            lies the root, which the host's sources declared as cells
            before the first step of a reader, the language's first; a
            query, a cell of a session and a source of the root are
            written alike. Rests on values and the pipeline; the verbs are
            names that run. ::link~(::binding | doc) ::link~(::bind | doc)
            ::link~(::qlang/verbs | doc)

            `env` lists what the reader declared, `kinds` the kinds the
            scope reads, and what lies under a noun `manifest` lists. A
            name reads its record by `binding`. A name declared inside a
            group closes with it, and which names a quote reads the
            chapter of code tells. A name declared under a label resides
            on it: it serves the values of the label, found by its name
            through the walk of their tags, and refuses any other subject
            under its address; a quote taken from one label and declared
            under another resides on the second. A name is declared once
            in a scope and again in a scope inside it, which shadows it
            for the steps after; a value the reader names takes any name,
            and a step that calls the name on a subject a word of that
            name answers is refused, so the two never meet unseen, the
            word called by its address there, `[1 2] | vec/count`; an
            address the root holds is declared once at all, so a reader
            extends a noun of the root and replaces none of its nodes. A
            word the reader declares under a label shadows a word of its
            name on a broader label in the reader's own calls, since the
            walk of a subject's tags reaches the narrower first, and no
            word of the root sees it: a word of the root runs in the
            root's scope, where a name under a noun of the root reads the
            root alone. A name begins with a letter of any script or `_`,
            and a word of a host, which reads or writes outside the
            language, is named as any word is. The docs in the slot of a
            declaration, between its name and its body, are its page, one
            doc each. A name under a label resolves its names through the
            tree of names as it stands at the call, itself among them, so
            it recurses by name, and the frames of a runaway meet a
            budget.
        ~~|
        #[::binding]
    | proves :names/listsUnderANoun
        |~~ `manifest` lists what lies under a noun. ~~|
        ~(::vec | manifest | has ::vec/count)
    | proves :names/closesWithItsGroup
        |~~ A name declared in a group closes with it. ~~|
        ~(1 | (:inner 2 | inner) | env | has :inner | not)
    | proves :names/servesTheValuesOfItsLabel
        |~~ A name under a label serves its values. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/twice ~(get /w | mul 2) | ::Box{:w 3} | twice | eq 6)
    | proves :names/extendsANounOfTheLanguage
        |~~ A reader's name under `vec` serves vectors. ~~|
        ~(:vec/second ~(get /1) | [7 8 9] | second | eq 8)
    | proves :names/refusesAnotherSubject
        |~~ Another subject is refused under the name's address. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/w2 ~(get /w | mul 2) | {:w 3} | Box/w2 !| type | eq ::Box/w2/SubjectNotBoxError)
    | proves :names/residesWhereDeclared
        |~~ A quote taken from one label resides on the one it is declared
            under.
        ~~|
        ~(:A |~~ A. ~~| | :A/f ~(1) | :B |~~ B. ~~| | :B/g (::A/f | binding | get /value) | get [~(::B{} | g) ~(::A{} | B/g !| type)] | eq [1 ::B/g/SubjectNotBError])
    | proves :names/needsItsNoun
        |~~ A name under no declared noun is refused. ~~|
        ~(:Box/area ~(1) !| type | eq ::bind/NounNotDeclaredError)
    | proves :names/declaresOnce
        |~~ A name is declared once in a scope. ~~|
        ~(:k 1 | :k 2 !| type | eq ::bind/NameDeclaredTwiceError)
    | proves :names/meetsAWordAloud
        |~~ A value named as a word meets the word aloud where the subject
            answers it.
        ~~|
        ~(:count 5 | get [~(7 | count) ~([1 2] | count !| type)] | eq [5 ::call/ValueMeetsWordError])
    | proves :names/shadowsForTheReaderAlone
        |~~ A reader's word shadows a broader one in the reader's call,
            and a word of the root never sees it.
        ~~|
        ~(:vec/doc ~("mine") | [1 2] | get [~(doc) ~(tutorial | first | type)] | eq ["mine" ::doc])
    | proves :names/beginsWithALetter
        |~~ A name begins with a letter or `_`, and `@` begins none. ~~|
        ~("@out" | parse !| type | eq ::quote/ParseError)
    | proves :names/holdsEachDocOfItsSlot
        |~~ Two docs in the slot are two parts of its page. ~~|
        ~(:x |~~ One. ~~| |~~ Two. ~~| 1 | env | get /x | get /docs | count | eq 2)
    | proves :names/recursesByName
        |~~ A name calls itself through the tree of names. ~~|
        ~(:map/walk ~(get {:n /n :kids ~(get /kids | map ~walk)}) | {:n 1 :kids [{:n 2 :kids []}]} | walk | get /kids/0/n | eq 2)
    | tells :verbs
        |~~ Verbs. A verb lives on a kind, the one whose values it serves,
            and has an address, `::vec/sort`; so what can be done with a
            value is asked of its kind, and one word serves many kinds.
            The verbs of a kind and those of every value are the whole of
            them: a verb in neither list does not exist. Rests on values
            and the pipeline; errors name the verb that refused.
            ::snippet~(::vec | manifest) lists the verbs of a kind and
            ::snippet~(::any | manifest) those of every value.
            ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)

            A name finds its verb through the kind of its subject: its
            tags from the outside in, then the shape beneath them, then
            the verbs every value answers, on `::any`; a verb of vectors
            reaches a vector under a tag and keeps the tag. A word is a
            function named under a label, built from `::fn` by commands
            with its recipe last,
            `:number/plus (::fn | takes :by |~~ How much. ~~| /0 ::number | impl ~(add by))`:
            `on` names its subject, `takes` an input with its page, the
            place of the call it reads and its kinds, `returns` its
            answer, `throws` a refusal, `carries` a fact it carries,
            `holds` an invariant and `proves` a law, and its call reads
            them from the function, each input bound by its name in the
            recipe, a name the recipe declares no second time; `manifest`
            answers them before a call. A recipe written alone under a
            label, `:number/double ~(mul 2)`, is the function of no
            commands it is, which takes no object; an input, an answer, a
            subject and an invariant are written in the chain alone. An
            answer of the word's own label goes back under the tags the
            subject came through. A modifier is evaluated at the call
            against the subject, the value in the pipe, which is always
            the subject of the call; a word that combines several values
            of one subject reads each from its args, `mulOf /price /qty`;
            an input that runs code later takes
            a quote, `~name` or `~(get /path)` the quote of one step, and
            a condition is a boolean. A kind is a label built from
            `::kind` by commands and declared under a name,
            `:Box |~~ A parcel. ~~| (::kind | reads :w |~~ The width. ~~| /w ::number)`:
            `wraps` names its payload and its kinds, `reads` a field the
            payload holds at a place and its kinds, `holds` an invariant
            of the value or of a field, `throws` a refusal, `carries` a
            fact and `proves` a law, and a kind of the core `impl` the
            check its host hands; the constructor attaches the tag to the
            payload or refuses it, and builds no other value, so every
            value put under it keeps them, and a label declared by its
            page alone is a kind of no commands. A tag no declaration
            answers is a kind of identity, which checks nothing and lays
            the tag on the value. A word answers under the label its
            `returns` names. A word of a noun itself, whose subject `on`
            names `::tag`, takes the tag name as its subject, so a noun
            that stands for a thing outside the language is addressed
            bare, `::Jenkins | builds`, while a word of the noun's values
            refuses the tag name. A word is told as a story, in one order:
            the word and its page, its inputs, its answer, its refusals,
            its laws and its recipe, one chain; a kind as its page, its
            payload, its fields, its refusals, its invariants, its laws
            and its recipe; and the refusal of a step as its page, which
            repairs the step, its facts and its laws. `source` reads the
            story of a name as it is written, `::vec/sort | source`.
        ~~|
        #[::any ::fn ::holds ::kind]
    | proves :verbs/findsTheVerbOfTheKind
        |~~ `sort` finds the verb of vectors. ~~|
        ~([3 1 2] | sort | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :verbs/keepsTheTag
        |~~ A verb of vectors reaches a vector under a tag and keeps the
            tag.
        ~~|
        ~(::Box[3 1 2] | sort | eq ::Box[1 2 3])
    | proves :verbs/declaresTheInputs
        |~~ `query` declares its path and its default as its inputs. ~~|
        ~(::any/query | manifest | eq #[::any/query/default ::any/query/path])
    | proves :verbs/answersUnderTheTags
        |~~ A sum goes back under the tag the subject came through. ~~|
        ~(::Width#2 | add 3 | eq ::Width#5)
    | proves :verbs/isAFunctionNamedUnderALabel
        |~~ A word is a function named under a label. ~~|
        ~(:number/plus (::fn | takes :by |~~ How much. ~~| /0 ::number | impl ~(add by)) | 5 | plus 2 | eq 7)
    | proves :verbs/takesNoObjectForARecipeWrittenAlone
        |~~ A recipe written alone takes no object. ~~|
        ~(:number/double ~(mul 2) | 5 | get [~(double) ~(double 3 !| type)] | eq [10 ::number/double/ArgsBeyondInputsError])
    | proves :verbs/combinesValuesOfOneSubject
        |~~ A word that combines several values of one subject reads each
            from its args.
        ~~|
        ~({:price 100 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty | eq 300)
    | proves :verbs/takesAQuoteForLaterCode
        |~~ Code run later is a quote. ~~|
        ~([{:age 3} {:age 20}] | filter ~(get /age | gt 18) | count | eq 1)
    | proves :verbs/takesAPathAsCode
        |~~ A path is the code that reads it from each element,
            `sort /age`.
        ~~|
        ~("[{:age 3}] | sort /age | first | get /age" | parse | apply it | eq 3)
    | proves :verbs/needsABoolean
        |~~ A condition is a boolean. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | filter ~(1) !| type | eq ::vec/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError)
    | proves :verbs/laysAnUndeclaredTag
        |~~ A tag no declaration answers checks nothing. ~~|
        ~(::Small#12 | payload | eq 12)
    | proves :verbs/answersUnderTheKindItReturns
        |~~ A word answers under the kind its `returns` names. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/half (::fn | returns |~~ The half. ~~| ::number | impl ~(get /0 | div 2)) | ::Box[4] | half | eq 2)
    | proves :verbs/takesTheTagOfItsNoun
        |~~ A word of a noun itself takes the tag name as its subject. ~~|
        ~(:Desk |~~ A help desk. ~~| | :Desk/hours (::fn | on |~~ The desk itself. ~~| ::tag | impl ~("9 to 5")) | ::Desk | hours | eq "9 to 5")
    | proves :verbs/refusesTheTagForAWordOfValues
        |~~ A word of the noun's values refuses the tag name. ~~|
        ~(:Desk |~~ A help desk. ~~| | :Desk/size ~(count) | ::Desk | size !| type | eq ::Desk/size/SubjectNotDeskError)
    | proves :verbs/keepsTheInvariantsOfItsKind
        |~~ A value put under a kind keeps the kind's invariants. ~~|
        ~(:Pos |~~ A positive number. ~~| (::kind | holds :NotPositiveError |~~ A number above zero. ~~| ~(gt 0)) | ::Pos#-3 !| type | eq ::Pos/NotPositiveError)
    | tells :errors
        |~~ Errors. A failure does not throw: a step that fails answers an
            error, a value that carries the facts of the failure, the path
            it took and the tag of the site that refused, which names the
            page of its procedure. A reader learning a tool fails often,
            and the failure is where what it needs is cheapest to give.
            Rests on values, the pipeline and verbs; the hypertext holds
            the pages the sites name.
            ::link~(::error | doc) ::link~(::fail | doc)
            ::link~(::explanation | doc) ::link~(::qlang/code | doc)

            A failed step answers a failure the tag of its site names. `|`
            passes a failure by, and `map` answers the first an element
            answers. `a !| b` alone catches a failure, running `b` on the
            error at rest, a value like any other, and recovers each
            failure where it is read. No value holds a failure: a template
            whose quote fails, a literal whose constructor refuses its
            payload and a name whose body fails answer the failure. The facts name
            what refused, and the first stop of the trail holds the value
            the step refused. `explain` shows the page of the site above
            the error. The trail is written as the failure flies: a stop
            for each level it left, and the last stop the steps it
            skipped, a quote that runs again as held code runs. An error
            at rest raised
            again resumes the trail it holds, and one without `:trail`
            starts it over. Whether the step before failed is
            `false !| true`.
        ~~|
        #[::error ::fail ::explanation]
    | proves :errors/namesTheSite
        |~~ The error's tag names its site. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | proves :errors/recoversWhereRead
        |~~ `!|` inside the code `map` runs recovers each failure. ~~|
        ~([1 "x"] | map ~(mul 2 !| 0) | eq [2 0])
    | proves :errors/holdsTheRefusedValue
        |~~ The first stop holds the refused value. ~~|
        ~("a" | mul 2 !| get [/actualType /trail/0/subject] | eq [::string "a"])
    | proves :errors/stopsAtEachLevel
        |~~ The trail stops at each level the error left. ~~|
        ~(:f ~(add "x" | mul 2) | 5 | apply f | sub 3 !| get /trail | map /step | eq [~(add "x") ~(apply f)])
    | proves :errors/holdsTheSkippedSteps
        |~~ The last stop holds the steps skipped. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| get /trail/-1/skipped | parse | eq "mul 2")
    | proves :errors/runsTheSkippedStepsAgain
        |~~ The steps a failure skipped run again by `apply`. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| get /trail/-1/skipped | :rest it | 5 | apply rest | eq 10)
    | proves :errors/resumesItsTrail
        |~~ A map raised again resumes its trail. ~~|
        ~(!{:kind :oops} | raise | count !| raise | add 1 !| get /trail | map /step | eq [~(raise)])
    | proves :errors/asksWhetherTheStepBeforeStood
        |~~ `ok !| false` answers whether the step before stood. ~~|
        ~(get [~(!{:kind :oops} | raise | ok !| false) ~(42 | ok !| false)] | eq [false true])
    | tells :code
        |~~ Code. A quote, `~(…)`, holds steps unrun as a value: a verb
            takes one where it needs code, `filter`, `sort`, `if`, `apply`
            runs one on a subject, and a quote is taken apart and built
            like any vector of steps. Because code is a value, the laws of
            a name run, a page holds links that open, and the trail of an
            error holds the steps it skipped, a quote that runs again.
            Rests on values and the pipeline; the hypertext is written in
            it.
            ::link~(::quote | doc) ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | doc)
            ::link~(::call/ValueWithheldError | doc)

            A quote written in a step, what a word takes or a part of a
            template it takes, is code of that step: the word runs it in
            the scope of the step that wrote it, and so does a word written
            in the language, by whatever road its recipe hands the quote
            on, since a call keeps where each of its objects came from.
            Any other code is held as data, a quote in a name, a field or
            a page, a law and a snippet among them, and runs in a lane of
            its own: the words, the nodes and the labels of the scope of
            its step and the names it declares itself, a value that scope
            names withheld from it and refused under its name,
            `::call/ValueWithheldError`. A value held code needs is an
            input of a function, `apply f [4]`, or is written into it by
            `fill`.
        ~~|
        #[::quote]
    | proves :code/runsByApply
        |~~ A quote held as a value runs by `apply`. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | :q it | 5 | apply q | eq 12)
    | proves :code/isAVectorOfSteps
        |~~ A quote counts its steps. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | count | eq 2)
    | proves :code/readsTheStepThatWroteIt
        |~~ A quote written in a step reads the names of that step. ~~|
        ~(:k 10 | 5 | apply ~(add k) | eq 15)
    | proves :code/withholdsAValueOfTheReader
        |~~ Held code reads no value the reader named. ~~|
        ~(:k 10 | :q ~(add k) | 5 | apply q !| get [~(type) /name] | eq [::call/ValueWithheldError :k])
    | proves :code/readsTheStepThroughAWordOfTheLanguage
        |~~ A word written in the language hands a quote on from the step
            that wrote it.
        ~~|
        ~(:k 10 | :vec/twice (::fn | takes :q |~~ The code. ~~| /0 ::quote | impl ~(map q | map q)) | [1 2] | twice ~(add k) | eq [21 22])
    | tells :hypertext
        |~~ The hypertext. The names are a space of addresses, `::`
            beginning an absolute one, `::vec/sort`, and a verb passes
            from an address along one of its axes, as a path reaches a
            file: `doc` to its page, `binding` to its record, `source` to
            the code written under it, and `runLaws` runs the laws under
            it. An address holds no one entity but a set of
            values, and each passage reads one of them; the address, the
            verb and what it reaches are all values, so the pipeline goes
            on from what an axis reaches as from any value, and a tag you
            declare with verbs on it adds axes of its own. A keyword after
            `doc` is an anchor within the page of its subject,
            `::qlang/pipeline | doc`, a vector of anchors a path, a set of
            anchors several pages at once. A page is a doc, prose that
            holds code: a quote under `::link` is a link to another page,
            and a quote under `::snippet` a query a reader runs to see
            what it shows, `open` running either. So a page leads on to
            the pages that go deeper, and the pipeline moves through them
            as through any data: `manifest` lists what lies under a noun,
            its verbs and refusals, `links | map ~open` opens every page a
            page names in one query, and a filter over pages by
            `content | contains` searches them. Nothing is read that was
            not asked for, and a query names the shape of what it reads.
            The laws that prove a name stand under it, which `runLaws`
            holds to answer true. Rests on every concept before it.
            ::link~(::doc | doc) ::link~(::link | doc) ::link~(::snippet | doc)
            ::link~(::qlang/hosts | doc)
        ~~|
        #[::doc ::link ::snippet ::law]
    | proves :hypertext/opensTheLinksOfAPage
        |~~ The links of a page open in one query. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | doc | links | map ~open | count | gt 0)
    | proves :hypertext/passesIntoThreeAxes
        |~~ One address passes into its page, its record and its source. ~~|
        ~(::vec/take | get [~(doc | type) ~(binding | type) ~(source | type)] | eq [::doc ::binding ::quote])
    | proves :hypertext/searchesThePages
        |~~ A filter over pages finds `drop`. ~~|
        ~(::vec | manifest | filter ~(doc | content | contains "after the first") | eq #[::vec/drop])
    | tells :hosts
        |~~ Hosts. The language runs inside a host, a tool that embeds it
            and brings verbs of its own, each a name whose value is
            `::builtin{}`; the command line is one. A value is whole in
            the language, and the host prints a value whole unless a
            reader asks for a budget, by `elide` in the query or by the
            host's flag, the command line's `--budget=N`: an answer whose
            print fits the budget stays whole, and what a budget left out
            is an `::elision` marker, whose `:read` continues the query to
            the part. A failure the host prints is an alert: its tag and
            its facts, the subject where it arose within an excerpt, and a
            marker for each subject it passed, the failure itself staying
            whole; the command line writes below it the prose of its
            refusal's page, as `!| explain` reads it, so the reader
            repairs the call where it failed. The command line reads JSON
            from stdin as values and other text as a string, and prints
            its answer as the language prints it, a string as its text, so
            `| json` writes JSON; its page, `::cli | doc`, tells the rest.
            Rests on values and the hypertext.
            ::link~(::elision | doc) ::link~(::any/elide | doc)
            ::link~(::builtin | doc)
        ~~|
        #[::elision ::builtin]

